.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NSHD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nshd
.Nd network configuration daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bdv
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads the system's configuration for
.Xr nsh 8 ,
which requests it over a socket.
It is privilege separated.
The parent process, as root, binds the sockets, starts the children,
and accepts each client of the read-write socket.
Each child except system runs as a user of its own, so that no child can
signal a child of another role or reach its files:
.Bl -tag -width "frontend"
.It show
Four show processes, each as _nshshow in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
answer the read-only socket.
They answer only with what anyone may see: interfaces and their
addresses, routes and route lookups, the ARP and neighbor tables,
VLANs, bridges, the kernel statistics that need no root, the IPsec
flows, the daemons that run, the version, who is logged in and the
hostname.
They send these as facts, which the client renders.
A long answer delays only the show that makes it, and the show makes it
only as the client reads it.
A show has no pipe to the other children.
It is not
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d ,
because no promise allows the interface ioctls that it uses.
Each opens
.Pa /var/run/utmp
before its
.Xr chroot 2 .
.It frontend
One for each client of the read-write socket, each as _nshfront in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
in a process group of its own.
Each client is a session, which sends one request at a time.
The parent accepts the client, reads its uid and gid from its socket,
and gives the session a number that it never uses again.
It gives config a pipe to the session's frontend, and gives system a
copy of the client's socket.
Once both have taken the session, the parent starts the frontend with
that pipe and the client's socket, and no other descriptor.
config knows which session a message is for by the pipe it comes on, so
a frontend can speak only for its own session.
A frontend is
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d
to use only the descriptors it has and to pass on a terminal's pty, so
it can signal no process outside its own process group.
When the client goes, its frontend ends, config drops the session, and
the parent tells system to drop it too.
system also checks each second that each session's client is still
connected.
When it finds one gone, or drops a session for another reason, the
parent kills the session's frontend if it still runs, and config drops
the session too.
So a frontend that stays after its client has gone keeps the session
for about a second at most.
A frontend that ends, or sends config what it may not, ends only its
session.
config also drops a session that has 16 requests unfinished and makes
another, since a client makes one request at a time and watches at most
four logs.
The parent takes at most 64 sessions at once, and closes a client past
that.
config takes at most 1024 requests unfinished from all sessions, and
refuses a request past that.
A request counts until system ends it, even after its session has
ended.
When a session ends, system ends each of its requests that waits for a
commit and has not begun.
A client with _nshweb's uid is the web configurator's session.
Only that session may log in, as a user, with that user's password, and
request the user's terminal.
A login that is refused is answered only that it is incorrect, and
system logs why, with the user's name.
.It config
Runs as _nshconf in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
holds the sessions, and requests from system what they need.
A session has the permissions of its user's login class (see
.Cm login class
in
.Xr nsh 8 ) .
config requests them from system at the session's hello.
system answers for the uid of the session's client, which it reads from
the client's socket (see system, below).
config allows each request only with its permission: a show needs
view, an operation operate, and configure, a commit, a rollback, a load
or a daemon's rules configure.
nsh checks exec, the permission to run programs, itself; config only
reports whether the class has it.
.Cm configure
and
.Cm configure exclusive
also need admin, because their candidate is shared by every session in
them.
A session without admin sees no password hash of a
.Cm login user
line.
The web configurator's session has no permissions until it logs in.
It then becomes the session of the user it logged in as, with that
user's uid and the permissions of the user's class.
config checks a session's requests against the permissions the session
has after its hello, or its login; system checks those it runs against
the permissions of the user's class as it is then.
A commit that changes login users or classes ends only a web
configurator's session whose user can no longer log in, and a session
whose hello config has not yet answered.
Before its script runs, config lowers the permissions of each session
that would lose one by it to those the session would have after, and
nshd takes new sessions again once the commit ends.
A session in
.Cm configure
or
.Cm configure exclusive
that loses admin may then only leave it.
nsh checks the exec permission itself, from the permissions the session
had after its hello, until it connects again.
A permission its class gains counts at once in system's checks, as for
the admin lines of a commit, and in config's once the session connects
again.
The commit runs to its end, and
.Cm show rollback
says how it went.
A rollback of an unconfirmed commit that changes login users or
classes leaves every session running, as it takes back a commit: a
permission it
takes away counts at once in system's checks, and in config's once the
session connects again.
system decides which commits and rollbacks change them, by comparing
the login lines of the configuration before with those of the
configuration after.
It decides which sessions would lose a permission by comparing the
permissions each session has now with those its user would have by the
login lines after.
Such a commit runs once config has lowered those sessions' permissions,
and a rollback once config says it takes no new session.
If config has not done so within 10 seconds, system acts as it does
when config exits, as below.
Until the commit or rollback ends, a web login is refused, and the web
configurator logs in again.
A client whose session ended connects again, with the permissions its
class has then.
A session in
.Cm configure private
has a candidate of its own; the sessions in
.Cm configure
share one, and each sees what the others change in it.
config edits a candidate a line at a time, with nsh's own commands, as
they run in
.Cm configure private .
The running configuration, and the kernel state that the commands read,
come from a snapshot that system takes when a session enters configure
or shows its candidate.
.Cm configure exclusive
is
.Cm configure
while no other session changes the shared candidate or commits any
candidate.
It is refused while another session has it, or while another user has
changes to the shared candidate that are not committed.
Leaving it, or closing its session, discards the changes it did not
commit.
While a commit of the shared candidate runs, no other session changes
it.
Commits run one at a time, across all the sessions.
config plans a commit from a snapshot and checks it.
config then hands the commit to system, with the daemons' rules that it
changes.
system first tests the rules, and begins the commit only if every test
passes.
Then system runs the commit to its end: it records the commit and
runs its script.
system then checks that the running configuration is the candidate, and
that no line of the script failed.
If so, system saves the running configuration; if not, system runs a
script that rolls the changes back, in a job of its own, and checks
that the running configuration is again what it was.
.Cm commit check
stops after the tests.
.Cm commit confirmed
runs its script even if the candidate is the running configuration.
If the running configuration is then the candidate, it stays in place,
not saved, until the deadline; a commit that saves confirms it.
A commit outside configure only has system save the running
configuration.
In
.Cm configure terminal ,
the session's candidate is made from the running configuration when
each line arrives.
Each line that changes the candidate is committed, but not saved;
commit there saves the running configuration.
.Ic nsh Fl c
sends its configuration a part at a time.
config makes it the session's own candidate, in place of the running
configuration, and commits all of it or none of it.
A session in configure, but not in
.Cm configure terminal ,
may set a daemon's rules for a routing table in its candidate, as the
daemon's
.Cm edit
sets them.
It sends the rules a part at a time, up to 1 MB in all, and it may also
request them.
.Cm rollback Ar N
loads configuration
.Ar N ,
which system reads, into the candidate; rollback 0 loads the running
configuration.
Once system has begun a commit, the commit runs to its end even if its
session ends.
system begins it only while the session's client is still connected.
A session with view may watch commits, and interfaces and routes.
config then sends it unrequested notices between its replies to the
session's requests.
The notices report a commit saved, an unconfirmed commit with its
minutes, a line of
.Cm configure terminal
committed, and
.Cm configure exclusive
entered or left, each with the user's uid.
They also report an unconfirmed commit rolled back at its deadline, as
system tells config.
They report that interfaces or addresses changed, and which routing
tables' routes changed, from a
.Xr route 4
socket that the parent opens for config, once a second at most.
A session may also watch logs.
system follows each log, and config sends the session the text that
system reads from it as notices, until the session's watch drops the
log.
A client that completes and runs lines itself, as
.Xr nsh 8
does, may request nsh's command tables and the names of interfaces, groups,
bridges and routing tables, if its session has view.
Each row of the tables says how its command runs outside configure and
in it.
The names come from config's last snapshot; if there is none yet,
config takes one first.
config is
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d
to stdio, recvfd and sendfd.
It reads no kernel state, opens no file and runs no program of its own,
and refuses a line that would.
.It system
Runs as root, and reads the configuration from the kernel, the
daemons and the saved configuration.
Whoever started
.Nm ,
system has only the environment that
.Xr rc 8
gives,
.Ev PATH
.Pa /sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
and
.Ev HOME
.Pa / ,
with umask 022 and working directory
.Pa / ,
and the programs it runs have the same.
Only system writes the saved configuration,
.Pa /var/db/nsh/nshrc ;
nsh's
.Cm show startup-config
requests it.
It answers config and no one else.
config names the session of each request it sends, and system acts on
a request only while that session's client is still connected.
system reads the uid it records, and whether a session is the web
configurator's, itself: it calls
.Xr getpeereid 2
on the socket of the session's client, which the parent passes it.
It answers config's request for a session's permissions from that uid
too.
A session whose client has closed its connection, or whose client's
socket system does not have, has none.
This shows which user a session's connection belongs to.
It does not show what that user asked for: system trusts config to name
the session a request came from.
So a compromised config could act in the name of any connected client,
but only as that client's login class allows.
Once a session's client has closed its connection, system refuses each
request of that session that it has not begun, whether or not the
session's frontend is still there.
An operation or a commit that system has begun can go on.
system takes a session only while enough descriptors stay free for its
own work: commits and the streams it runs.
When it cannot, the session ends before it begins.
system checks the permissions of that uid's login class itself, as
config does: an operation needs operate, and a commit, its test and a
save need configure.
It also checks a login by the web configurator itself.
The user must be named by a
.Cm login user
line and must not have uid 0.
.Xr auth_usercheck 3
checks the password, and
.Xr auth_approval 3
checks the account.
These run in a child process of system's, in a process group of its
own, so a slow authentication program delays nothing else that system
does.
At most four logins are checked at once, one for each session; another
is refused until one ends.
A check that takes more than 30 seconds fails, and system kills its
process group; the check counts against the four until then.
Once the check ends, system makes sure again that the session's client
is still connected and that the user may still log in, and takes the
permissions of the user's class as they are then.
The session then acts as that user until it ends, and its commits
record that user's uid.
system starts such a session's terminal as
.Xr login 1
starts a shell: it opens a pty, and runs
.Xr nsh 8
on it as the user, with the user's groups and
.Xr login.conf 5
settings, in the user's home directory and a clean environment.
system passes the pty to the session through config and frontend, and
nsh exits when the session closes it.
system runs an operation a session requests, such as
.Cm flush routes
or a daemon's action, if nsh's code accepts it as an operation.
It runs the operation as root, in a child of its own, and streams its
output as it streams a show's.
A child of system also prints what a daemon's control program prints
for a show, and the DHCP leases.
system streams that output only as fast as the client reads it; the
child, and its process group, are killed if the client disconnects
first.
The program is the one nsh's tables name for the show, never one a
client names.
The child runs it as _nshcheck, unless system lists it as a program that
keeps root because it uses a daemon's control socket,
.Pa /dev/pf
or PF_KEY, which are root's.
The child that prints the leases opens them, then becomes _nshcheck.
A log a session watches is followed the same way.
The child opens
.Pa /var/log/daemon ,
.Pa /var/log/messages
or
.Pa /var/log/authlog
as root, then becomes _nshcheck.
It prints the log's last lines, then each line as it arrives.
When
.Xr newsyslog 8
rotates the log, a new child follows the new file.
pf's log comes from
.Xr tcpdump 8
on
.Xr pflog 4 ;
tcpdump keeps root to open it, and gives up root itself.
system checks that the session's class has view, and admin for
authlog, as it checks an operation.
A commit's script, its save, and each test of a daemon's rules run in a
child of system.
If the child runs past its time limit, it fails: its process group gets
SIGTERM, then SIGKILL 10 seconds later, or 5 seconds later for a test.
The limit is 900 seconds for a script, 300 for a save and 120 for a
test.
A script and a save run as root.
config makes a commit's script into edits, and system runs the edits and
never the script's text.
Each edit names one of the commands a configuration has, by its place
among them, with the line's words, or, for a command whose fields
config reads from them, such as an interface's
.Ic mtu
or
.Ic description ,
with their values.
A line takes an address in numeric form only, so
.Nm
sends no DNS query and reads no DNS answer.
The command is a statement, an interface's or a bridge's line, the end
of a block, a daemon's rules, the state a daemon is left in, or what
follows its rules.
A line of any other command, such as
.Ic reboot ,
.Ic ping
or
.Ic \&! ,
or of a daemon's operation, such as smtp's
.Cm remove ,
has no edit.
config refuses a commit with such a line and reports each one; the boot
leaves each one out and reports it.
system checks every edit before it runs any, and runs the script only
if each edit names a command that exists, where it belongs, and each
value has its field's type and is within its bounds.
An edit that runs a daemon's program fails unless the program exits 0,
and then leaves the daemon's state as it was.
A disable's pkill may also exit 1, which says no such daemon runs.
pf's enable and disable turn pf on and off with pf's ioctls, and
succeed only if pf is then on or off.
An edit that fails makes the script fail.
A commit by a session without admin may keep a line that needs admin
(see
.Cm login class
in
.Xr nsh 8 )
only as it was running before the commit.
The parser checks such a commit's script before system records the
commit, and a commit it refuses runs nothing.
For such a session, the parser checks a test's rules for the same
lines, judged against the running configuration, before the test
runs.
A test runs as _nshcheck, with its rules in a directory of their own;
_nshcheck owns both.
The tests of relayd, ldapd, npppd, sasyncd and sshd are the exceptions:
they keep root, because these daemons read keys or secrets that their
rules name.
While a test, a script or a save runs, other requests wait, but streams
go on.
system keeps a record of a commit that has begun.
The commit removes the record when it saves or is rolled back.
A commit that does neither leaves the record, and the next commit
reports what it left.
While the record says a commit's changes were not saved, a commit may
only save the running configuration or restore the configuration from
before that commit.
An unconfirmed commit also writes a record of its own.
Its rollback configuration is what ran before the first unconfirmed
commit.
If the commit is rolled back, that record is restored to how it was
before the commit.
A commit that saves removes that record after the save succeeds.
The deadline is the commit's minutes, counted from when the commit
ends.
After the deadline, system rolls the commit back, once every commit it
began has ended and no script, save or test runs.
It demotes carp by 128, runs a script that rolls the changes back, and
promotes carp again once the running configuration matches the rollback
configuration.
If the rollback fails, or its script cannot start, system tries again
5 seconds later, up to three attempts in all.
Then the record says the rollback failed, and carp stays demoted
until a commit saves.
When
.Nm
starts, system finds any unconfirmed commit that an earlier run left,
and rolls it back once its deadline has passed.
A commit is refused if the running configuration is not the one it was
planned from.
system reads the running configuration itself as the commit begins,
and an undo, or a rollback of an unconfirmed commit, goes back to what
it read.
system runs in a session of its own: when
.Nm
ends, the parent kills every process left in that session, and every
process of _nshcheck's.
.It archive
Runs as _nsharc in a
.Xr chroot 2 .
It stores the shared candidate, and the users who changed it, for
config, in
.Pa /var/db/nsh/archive ,
which it opens before it enters the
.Xr chroot 2 .
config sends it the candidate each time the candidate changes.
config answers the session that changed the candidate once archive has
stored it, whole and synced to disk, or has reported that it did not.
When
.Nm
starts, config loads what archive stored before any session enters
.Cm configure .
.It parser
Runs as _nshparse in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
and may only read and write what it was given.
system starts a new one for each check, so a parser keeps nothing from
one check to the next.
It reads text for system, which reads none of it itself: for a session
without admin, the script of a commit and the rules of a test, against
the running configuration, and says each line that needs admin.
system passes on what the parser prints, and takes its exit status as
the verdict.
.El
.Pp
config and system, and config and archive, each talk on a pipe of
their own.
The parent makes the pipes before the children start, so each child
knows who sent what it reads.
Each child also has a pipe to the parent, which tells it when to
start, and on which config gets each new session's pipe and system the
session's client's socket.
The parent accepts only config's and system's word that they took a
session, system's word that config is to end, and system's message that
the boot is done.
.Pp
If any child but a frontend exits, or sends the parent or a peer a
message that it may not send,
.Nm
ends: the child's peers find their pipes closed and exit, and the parent
kills the rest with
.Dv SIGKILL .
No child is started again.
config is the exception: when config exits, or sends what it may not,
system first finishes what it began, and then
.Nm
ends.
First, system asks the parent to end config, which may still run, and
the parent kills config, the frontends and every child but system with
.Dv SIGKILL .
What system finishes is a commit that it has begun, with its undo or
its save, a rollback of an unconfirmed commit, or the boot, each within
its own time limit.
A commit that changes login users or classes, and still waits for
config to lower or end sessions, ends as one undone, without running.
A rollback that still waits so runs once the parent has reaped each
child it killed, and says so.
Until then
.Nm
takes no clients, and system takes no requests.
system drops a commit it has not begun, such as one whose tests run.
A signal to end
.Nm
still ends it at once.
If
.Nm
ends while an unconfirmed commit waits, nothing rolls the commit back
until
.Nm
is started again.
This holds for an unconfirmed commit that system kept after config
exited too.
.Pp
With
.Fl b ,
as rc starts it,
.Nm
boots.
Before the parent and the shows accept clients, system makes the
running configuration the saved one, as much of it as takes effect, as
.Xr nsh 8
.Fl i
does.
What the checks find is reported, and each daemon's rules are tested,
and the boot goes on whatever they find.
What system prints for the boot is logged.
If a rollback of an unconfirmed commit runs as
.Nm
starts, the boot waits for it to end.
.Nm
returns once the boot is done, with 0 if the running configuration is
then the saved configuration, else 1.
The first time, when there is no saved configuration, system copies
.Pa /etc/nshrc
to the saved configuration.
system keeps a record of the boot, which names the host boot that it is
for.
An
.Nm
started again during the same host boot runs the boot again only if the
boot did not finish or failed.
carp is demoted by 128 until the boot succeeds, or, if it fails, until a
commit saves.
A boot that succeeds removes the record of an unconfirmed commit,
because the running configuration is then its rollback configuration.
If
.Nm
ends during the boot, the boot does not finish, and the next
.Nm
started during the same host boot runs it again.
.Pp
Each child's
.Xr chroot 2
is its user's home directory.
.Nm
starts only if each user, and _nshcheck, exists, is not root, and has a
uid of its own, and only if _nshweb, the web configurator's user, when
it exists, is not root and has a uid of its own.
.Pp
Only root and the members of the group nshadmin may connect to the
read-write socket.
Every session there whose login class has view may see the running
configuration.
The read-only socket is for anyone.
.Pp
system does as root what config requests of it.
So a compromised config may request anything that a session may, in a
commit that a session requested: config is trusted as much as the users
of the read-write socket are.
After starting the children, the parent stays root only to stop
system's leftover processes when
.Nm
ends.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b
Boot, as above.
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize, and log to standard error.
.It Fl v
Produce more verbose output.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/run/nshd.ro.sockXX" -compact
.It Pa /etc/nshrc
a first configuration, which system saves when there is no saved
configuration
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/nshd
the
.Xr rc.d 8
script, which starts
.Nm
with
.Fl b
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.sock
the read-write socket, mode 0660, group nshadmin
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.ro.sock
the read-only socket, mode 0666
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/nshrc
the saved configuration, root's
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/nshrc.N
copies of the saved configurations, for rollback; rollback 0 is the
newest
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/login
the running login classes and users, and which accounts
.Nm
made
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/commit
the commit record
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/confirm
the record of an unconfirmed commit
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/boot
the record of the boot
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/archive/candidate
the shared candidate, as archive stores it
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nsh 8 ,
.Xr nshdeck 8 ,
.Xr rc.d 8
